From 840c82037ca570657c6773ea30ca57e8e2549752 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lakitu12 <1219387824@qq.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 18:38:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] um: umfb -- a framebuffer whose pixels are mirrored to a host
 sink

A UML guest draws its screen into /dev/fb0 -- redroid's SurfaceFlinger
through gralloc, Xorg through the fbdev driver, weston through its
fbdev backend -- but the guest cannot reach any host display API, and
the host cannot read the guest's kernel memory.  What both sides CAN
read is a shared file, so that is the transport: umfb registers an
ordinary fixed-mode fbdev device for the guest and mirrors the changed
pixels into a sink (a path, or an inherited descriptor such as the
Android app's memfd).

The pump is a UML helper thread, the same shape as ubd's io thread and
under the same discipline (plain memory and host calls only, no
printk, no allocation on it).  It diffs the framebuffer against the
sink and copies only the differing 64x64 tiles, then publishes the
dirty bitmap and bumps a release-stored seq counter.  Diffing the
whole buffer is the point, not a shortcut: a compositor writes through
its own mmap of /dev/fb0, which no driver callback can observe, so any
"hook the writes" design silently misses the only writer that matters.

The wire format (umfb.h: 1 KB header + pixels, magic and version
gated) is plain C, and the readers live outside this tree -- umview on
Linux (tools/um-arm64/umview), the console app's display screen on
Android -- so the file is the interface, not this driver.

Dormant unless the command line carries:

    umlfb=<path|fd:N>:<W>x<H>[-32][,hz=N][,tile=N]

Measured (x86 UML, initramfs smoke): the fb0 probe read back the mode
and the R,G,B,A byte layout; a base fill and shapes written through
the guest's mmap AND one pixel through pwrite() both landed in the
sink byte-exact; the pump published seq=1 with exactly the expected
20/20 dirty tiles; the run powers off cleanly with the pump thread
killed by its exitcall.
